{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/544797-heretical-thoughts-on-rat","id":544797,"title":"Heretical Thoughts on Rat","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-06-07T22:09:38-07:00","id":544797,"title":"Heretical Thoughts on Rat","content":"This is a long post, but I think it's worth it.<br /> <br /> I like taking the assumptions of the setting seriously and then find things that the authors did not intend. Rat as a Totem for the Garou is one of those. Rats are animals native to southeastern Asia. Rats did not spread into other lands until humanity developed enough to have agriculture, cities, and trade. For example, rats are known to not have reached Europe until the time of the Roman Empire. Brown Rats (also known as the Norwegian Rat) didn't reach Europe until the Middle Ages, and Ratkin are mostly tied to that species, not the black rat which arrived most earlier. I don't think most authors realized this. They assumed because Rats are universal around the world now, they must have always been so. They didn't realize how much the spread of rats across the world depended on humanity. Incidentally, Southeast Asia is also a region of the world where wolves did not exist.<br /> <br /> This has lots of interesting implications. Implications I don't believe any published material took seriously although it has occassionally been mentioned that rats originate in southeast Asia.<br /> <br /> First, it is extremely hard to imagine how the Bonegnawers could have become a Tribe until rats and wolves existed in the same place. I don't see how Garou could have selected Rat as a Totem when they had not ever run across his children in the Realm. This most likely points to India as being the original homeland of the Bonegnawers, and that the tribe could not have come into being very, very late in terms of history. At the very least, the Bonegnawers could not have existed for either the Impergium or the War of Rage. It also means that the Bonegnawers likely did not exist throughout much of the world until rats spread to those areas. Can you imagine some Bonegnawer explaing to a Fianna what his Totem is when no Fianna would ever have seen a single rat? Bonegnawers had to arrive in Europe quite late \u2013 not until well into historical times. No Fianna or Get of Fenris ever saw one in their native homelands until the Roman era or the Dark Ages. Most tribes probably had thousands of years of history where Bonegnawers did not even exist.<br /> <br /> Second, it implies some interesting things about the Ratkin and the War of Rage. To put it bluntly, the Garou are highly unlikely to have killed many of them other than some odd traveller who was very far from home. While it is entirely conceivable some random Garou used Moon Bridges to show up in southeast Asia and slaughter some Ratkin, it is extremely doubtful they stuck around long enough to really exterminate them. Ipso facto, someone else must have done so. We have lots of candidates \u2013 the most likely being the Bastet since southeast Asia is home to tigers (Khan) and leopards (Bagheera). However, bears (Gurahl), crocodiles (Mokole), snakes (Nagah), and just maybe foxes (Kitsune) were also in the same habitat. Isn't it more likely some of them slaughtered the Ratkin? The Garou no doubt slaughtered lots of the other Fera giving the habitat range of the wolf, but blaming the death of Ratkin is probably a smear. This fits into my idea posted lots of times earlier that the concept of a \u201cWar of Rage\u201d was created by historians well after the events in question.<br /> <br /> Third, the idea that Gaia gave them the role of controlling human population by eating their grain and spreading disease is just bizarre. For thousands of years of human history, rats did no such thing. Not only did it take long for humans to develop agriculture, but it took even longer for it to spread to southeast Asia so obviously the Ratkin's purpose was not to devour grain to induce famine and keep human population low. Furthermore, without large cities generating trash and sewage rats could live on, they wouldn't be very good at spreading disease. It's like saying that if Prarie Dog-People existed, their purpose is to limit human population, and if you don't see how Prarie Dog-People could do that, just wait several additional thousand years and it'll all make sense then. Whatever the original \u201cpurpose\u201d of the Ratkin \u2013 and I think any \u201cpurpose\u201d of the Changing Breeds are posthoc justifications of them asking themselves, \u201cwhy are we here\u201d - it couldn't have been to control human population. If they had a purpose at all, it's likely related to their small size and perchance to hide in trees and burrows. They are the spies of Gaia who were meant to go unseen and explore where others could not go (this would also explain why rats \u2013 and hence Ratkin \u2013 eventually cover most of the globe). This sounds like an overlap with the Bastet's purpose of learning secrets (since cats are supposed to be curious). This goes back into who ended up killing the Ratkin. If the local Bastet felt these small creatures were intruding on their domain, I can see them wiping out from spite. Given the connotations of what cats do to rodents, it makes perfect sense.<br /> <br /> Fourth, it is strange that Ratkin are considered to be a force of the Wyld. The only thing truly Wyld about them is 1) their fecundity, and 2) their ability to have established a wild animal habitat in the midst of human cities. If the Ratkin see themselves as a force to cull the human race by famine and disease, this clearly is not a function of the Wyld. It marks them as a minion of the destroyer \u2013 the Wyrm, even if they think they serve the old \u201cBalance Wyrm\u201d. I'm going to assume that the Ratkin <i>originally</i> were a force of the Wyld \u2013 their small size compensated by large birth litters and ability to flourish elsewhere than their original habitat to aid them in the mission to act as spies and go where the other changing breeds could not. However, the vast majority of Ratkin are now fallen, become corrupt, and are now effectively minions of the Wyrm. However, they don't know they are, and neither do the other Fera. Ratkin are in effect what the Children of Gaia are always afraid the Garou will become \u2013 so bound up in anger, despair, and hate that they fall to the Wyrm. Likely, the Ratkin are in that intermediate stage between the point of their fall and before they consciously chose to align themselves with the Wyrm. This is probably the same stage the White Howlers spent decades or even centuries before the tribe died and was reborn as the Black Spiral Dancers. It's just possible they can be redeemed, but too bad no one knows it. Everyone is so paralyzed by the equivalent of Garou liberal guilt and Changing Breed multiculturalism they can't admit what seems obvious to me \u2013 the Ratkin are traitors.<br /> <br /> Lastly, this implies some interesting things about Rat as a Totem. The books tell us that Bat fell to the Wyrm, but that it was effectively \u201cinsane\u201d for many years \u2013 devoted to both Gaia and the Wyrm schizophrenically until it finally fell. Rat is likely the same way. Since the Bonegnawers are the Children of Rat this has an important impact on them. We should see evidence of the Bonegnawers falling to the Wyrm. And we do see this. Such sentiments are found throughout the game line from many tribes who constantly complain about the Bonegnawers shirking their duty. I don't think the tribe overall has fallen as the Ratkin have, but they are approaching a major crisis within the tribe. Right now, the Wyrm taint is merely causing them to give up on their duties as Garou. It has not caused them to begin actively serving the Wyrm. However, we already know of the mechanism the Wyrm intends to use \u2013 the Maneaters. The Maneaters were large enough to be a f ull blown \u201ccamp\u201d within the tribe, and we know this \u201ccamp\u201d exists in many places and septs. While this is now downplayed in official material, we can imagine that the Maneaters is the eventual destiny of the Bonegnawers if they fall to the Wyrm. The Wyrm just needs time \u2013 perhaps even only a few more decades \u2013 for the seed to grow. It even explains why the Maneaters persist as a camp, its members truly believe Rat wants them to do this since Rat is in the process of falling just like Bat did.<br /> <br /> Those are my thoughts at least. Like many of my posts, I'm putting this on here not to argue I am right and others are wrong if they disagree with me. But to generate ideas some of you may find useful in your own games. But I am interested in hearing what you think.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-07T23:38:23-07:00","id":544856,"title":"","content":"RE: Nagah as a possible killer of Ratkin, the original kin of the Nagah were king cobras, the diet of which is given away in their genus name of <i>Ophiophagus</i>. They were originally of the stock of snakes that preyed primarily on other snakes, <b>including in large part snakes that preyed on rats</b>. The relationship between Nagah and Ratkin would likely be much, much more complex, and helps to understand why I hold the state of Nezumi having killed off most of the Japanese Nagah to be very, very ridiculous on so many levels. That entire appendix in W20 Changing Breeds was full of awful and grimdark above awful and grimdark. But I digress.<br /> <br /> RE: your fourth point, the Ratkin were not originally of the Wyld. They started &quot;falling&quot; to it after the War of Rage sent them underground. They aren't completely gone, but they do have Freak Aspects that are getting really close. Their totem might follow suit; the presentation in Bone Gnawers Revised has it split into the generous Rat Mother aspect and the apocalyptic and maddened Rat God aspect. Turning away from Gaia doesn't have to mean it's toward the Wyrm; just look at Shinzui turning to the Weaver completely in Apocalypse, after being teetering and tottering when introduced in Book of the Weaver.<br /> <br /> Looks like we have another error on par with the constant harping on dire wolves, in any case. Edit: This pretty much wrecks the Plague King and rat hosts from Forsaken, too, now that I think about it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":23,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","user":"Saur Ops Specialist","date":". 06-08-2015, 02:45 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-08T00:17:26-07:00","id":544887,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mautam\" target=\"_blank\">This</a> may also be worth noting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-08T02:35:20-07:00","id":544940,"title":"","content":"I can't help but think this is a bit too... scientific.<br /> <br /> While the <i>Rattus</i> genus originated in Asia and didn't spread to Europe until much later in history, rodents aren't exactly strangers to other places. Rodentia goes back to Laurasia (which split into North America, north Africa, Europe, and Asia), allowing rodents to spread to every modern continent besides Antarctica. A pre-historic Fianna might have never seen a <i>Rattus rattus</i> but they had plenty of rodents in their ecosystems. Early humans weren't exactly very picky about &quot;small rodents that eat grain and carry disease,&quot; to consider the ones with fur on their tails significantly different from the ones without.<br /> <br /> And it isn't like most Fianna ever ran across a griffin, unicorn, pegasus, or chimera running around in the wild... but those are all Tribal Totems.<br /> <br /> While the theory has a lot of interesting ideas, it feels way too reliant on the ancient Garou and spirits caring about extremely modern concepts like &quot;true rats&quot; needing to be from one particular lineage of rodents, rather than lumping dozens of lineages of rodents together and calling them all rats. The idea that the Bonegnawers couldn't exist in pre-history because 2-3 species of rats hadn't become so widespread yet; doesn't seem to really hold up when every inhabited continent has animals called rats on them that got there without human intervention.<br /> <br /> I think there's more solid territory in exploring two things:<br /> <br /> 1) Why are some of the Fera heavily associated with a small number of species while others are associated with vastly large taxonomical groups? Even without being scientific about it, it is remarkably odd that some are incredibly strict (Nuwisha are coyotes only) and others cover immensely diverse groups (Rokea are any sharks).<br /> <br /> 2) How exactly did the mundane animal associate Totems get where they are. How did Rat get to the top of its brood so fast compared to Mouse? How did Stag beat out Elk, or any other deer? What happened to put Owl in over Scarab?<br /> <br /> These seem to be much bigger holes in how the setting is described, and raise far more questions that could be interesting to answer, than getting picky about the taxonomy of the small furry things eating seeds and grains in ancient Sumer.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-08T08:01:46-07:00","id":545167,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/544797-heretical-thoughts-on-rat?p=544940#post544940\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I can't help but think this is a bit too... scientific.<br /> <br /> While the <i>Rattus</i> genus originated in Asia and didn't spread to Europe until much later in history, rodents aren't exactly strangers to other places. Rodentia goes back to Laurasia (which split into North America, north Africa, Europe, and Asia), allowing rodents to spread to every modern continent besides Antarctica. A pre-historic Fianna might have never seen a <i>Rattus rattus</i> but they had plenty of rodents in their ecosystems. Early humans weren't exactly very picky about &quot;small rodents that eat grain and carry disease,&quot; to consider the ones with fur on their tails significantly different from the ones without.</div> </div> </div> </div>But all of the pre-migration ones in Europe were mice, not rats. So some name changing or Bunyipping is going to need to happen there - subsidiary weremice, or a hop and jump from lineage to lineage like the Bunyip and Mokole.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And it isn't like most Fianna ever ran across a griffin, unicorn, pegasus, or chimera running around in the wild... but those are all Tribal Totems. </div> </div> </div>All mythical, all exempt from a great number of historical range concerns on account of not actually existing in real life.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> While the theory has a lot of interesting ideas, it feels way too reliant on the ancient Garou and spirits caring about extremely modern concepts like &quot;true rats&quot; needing to be from one particular lineage of rodents, rather than lumping dozens of lineages of rodents together and calling them all rats. The idea that the Bonegnawers couldn't exist in pre-history because 2-3 species of rats hadn't become so widespread yet; doesn't seem to really hold up when every inhabited continent has animals called rats on them that got there without human intervention. </div> </div> </div>Breeding barriers are, at the very least, going to be a concern without the means to get by them (see again Bunyip, Kucha Enkudu), since the Fera in question are going to be living as said animals and many limitations of form are still going to be present.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I think there's more solid territory in exploring two things:<br /> <br /> 1) Why are some of the Fera heavily associated with a small number of species while others are associated with vastly large taxonomical groups? Even without being scientific about it, it is remarkably odd that some are incredibly strict (Nuwisha are coyotes only) and others cover immensely diverse groups (Rokea are any sharks).<br /> <br /> 2) How exactly did the mundane animal associate Totems get where they are. How did Rat get to the top of its brood so fast compared to Mouse? How did Stag beat out Elk, or any other deer? What happened to put Owl in over Scarab?<br /> <br /> These seem to be much bigger holes in how the setting is described, and raise far more questions that could be interesting to answer, than getting picky about the taxonomy of the small furry things eating seeds and grains in ancient Sumer. </div> </div> </div>But those have extremely obvious metatextual reasons for being in the text that cut the knees out from searching for a textual reasoning. Why number one? The people writing and planning it thought that it was compelling. Why number two? Because, again, the people that created and contributed wanted it to be that way, and likely got very caught up in contemporary information that they hadn't known to have changed significantly over the proposed ancient timeline of the game. And right now, all of that would require heavy reliance on a Gift or Rite that was and is supposed to be rare and unusual to make the setting compatible with natural history, which, given many of its themes, it ought to concern itself with quite heavily.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-08T15:53:13-07:00","id":545909,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/544797-heretical-thoughts-on-rat?p=545167#post545167\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But all of the pre-migration ones in Europe were mice, not rats. So some name changing or Bunyipping is going to need to happen there - subsidiary weremice, or a hop and jump from lineage to lineage like the Bunyip and Mokole.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, lots of things, not just mice.<br /> <br /> My point is that the taxonomical differentiation between mice/rats/voles/packrats/kangroomice-rats/molerats/springhares/degu/etc. is an even more modern conceit. People for a very long time didn't really have all these different words. They came about as people started formal taxonomy. For example, in Semitic languages, the words for rat are generally just a variant on mouse. Sumerian (yay Forsaken?) doesn't seem to have a word for rat either, but had dozens of words for different kinds of mice; some of which might have been the true rats that lived in mountains to the east of Mesopotamia that the Sumerians probably had some knowledge of via cultural exchanges with the tribes there.<br /> <br /> While this doesn't do much about the points on the Ratkin being very clearly associated with true rats instead of wider swathes of rodentia like many of the other Fera, it easily explains how the Gnawers came about and got Rat as a Totem despite the <i>Rattus</i> genus having little (though some, given the true rats existed all the way into places like Kazakhstan, so there's overlap with Himalayan wolves) shared space with wolves.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> All mythical, all exempt from a great number of historical range concerns on account of not actually existing in real life. </div> </div> </div>Seems a bit of a cop out though; especially since the cWOD posits that these creatures did walk the physical world in some fashion. This would presumably correspond to the areas with the strongest cultural myths regarding them. If the Black Furies can have Pegasus, because as some point flying horses actually did exist in the north east Mediterranean areas, even if they never fly around where the Fianna might have seen them, the idea that a Fianna never saw a true rat in ancient times seems pretty irrelevant.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But those have extremely obvious metatextual reasons for being in the text that cut the knees out from searching for a textual reasoning. </div> </div> </div>This could just as easily be the response to the things brought up in the OP.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-08T16:54:05-07:00","id":546062,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/544797-heretical-thoughts-on-rat?p=545909#post545909\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Well, lots of things, not just mice.<br /> <br /> My point is that the taxonomical differentiation between mice/rats/voles/packrats/kangroomice-rats/molerats/springhares/degu/etc. is an even more modern conceit. People for a very long time didn't really have all these different words. They came about as people started formal taxonomy. For example, in Semitic languages, the words for rat are generally just a variant on mouse. Sumerian (yay Forsaken?) doesn't seem to have a word for rat either, but had dozens of words for different kinds of mice; some of which might have been the true rats that lived in mountains to the east of Mesopotamia that the Sumerians probably had some knowledge of via cultural exchanges with the tribes there.<br /> <br /> While this doesn't do much about the points on the Ratkin being very clearly associated with true rats instead of wider swathes of rodentia like many of the other Fera, it easily explains how the Gnawers came about and got Rat as a Totem despite the <i>Rattus</i> genus having little (though some, given the true rats existed all the way into places like Kazakhstan, so there's overlap with Himalayan wolves) shared space with wolves.</div> </div> </div> </div>Alright, but I don't think that Rokea should also be whales just because some ancient people didn't meaningfully distinguish between them and every other large sea creature.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Seems a bit of a cop out though; especially since the cWOD posits that these creatures did walk the physical world in some fashion. This would presumably correspond to the areas with the strongest cultural myths regarding them. If the Black Furies can have Pegasus, because as some point flying horses actually did exist in the north east Mediterranean areas, even if they never fly around where the Fianna might have seen them, the idea that a Fianna never saw a true rat in ancient times seems pretty irrelevant. </div> </div> </div>Those would have been spirits, not beings of flesh. Werewolf doesn't have Bygones. It's not its thing.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This could just as easily be the response to the things brought up in the OP. </div> </div> </div>Or we could cut the other way, see what's left standing, and build on that. I do like using fruits of research when they're available, and that was exactly the alteration made to the White Howlers in their tribebook, in stark contrast to 2e's REH Conan &quot;dwarfish brute&quot; Picts.<br /> <br /> Edit 2: Also WW seems to have lucked out with owls and the Owl totem - apparently it's considered an ill omen and harbinger of ill fortune around nearly the entire world. Fitting, for the Silent Striders.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":23,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","user":"Saur Ops Specialist","date":". 06-08-2015, 07:58 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-08T18:05:55-07:00","id":546207,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/544797-heretical-thoughts-on-rat?p=545167#post545167\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> But all of the pre-migration ones in Europe were mice, not rats. So some name changing or Bunyipping is going to need to happen there - subsidiary weremice, or a hop and jump from lineage to lineage like the Bunyip and Mokole.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm almost positive that the Ratkin can already breed with other rodents. I remember something being said about maybe a squirrel Ratkin in the breed book, but without the book on hand I don't have a reference page. I can try and look it up later when I get home.<br /> Plus the Ratkin aren't just limited to the brown rat, it just seems to be their favored breeding stock in the modern times, probably because of it's ability to thrive everywhere. It's likely that Ratkin bred with various rats (and possibly other rodents) and when the brown rat invaded and took over, they switched over to breeding with them almost exclusively. The section on rural Ratkin specifically addressed swamp rats and desert rats and mountain rats. It is possible that in more recent times (meant as all recorded history) that Ratkin have been most exclusively rats and during pre-history were more varied types of rodents.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://drive.google.com/uc?export=view&amp;id=0B9RzepSqCby3Mi1KUmJ5aWUwRVk\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/805-idpersona","name":"idpersona","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar805_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-08T18:53:15-07:00","id":546290,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/544797-heretical-thoughts-on-rat?p=544940#post544940\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While the theory has a lot of interesting ideas, it feels way too reliant on the ancient Garou and spirits caring about extremely modern concepts like &quot;true rats&quot; needing to be from one particular lineage of rodents, rather than lumping dozens of lineages of rodents together and calling them all rats. The idea that the Bonegnawers couldn't exist in pre-history because 2-3 species of rats hadn't become so widespread yet; doesn't seem to really hold up when every inhabited continent has animals called rats on them that got there without human intervention.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not unsympathetic to this, and I'm not trying to be scientific about it. I am trying to be consistent though. Rat to me are rats - regardless of whether they are black rats, brown rats, polynesian rats or anything else. However, Rat isn't &quot;all rodents&quot; which includes squirrels, porcupines, beavers hamsters, and other things that people don't conflate with one another. I think conflating rats and mice, which is what you are really talking about, is closer to the mark as people do confuse them and group them together. So it's a good point that &quot;Rat&quot; can be both the Totem of rats and mice. I don't think that's ever been stated in canon though (has it?) It's also true some species of rat are found elsewhere - yet clearly southeast Asia is home to most species and those that aren't are typically found in Africa (again, an area where wolves are not common).<br /> <br /> However, even here a lot of what I said applies. House Mice originally originated in southern or southeastern Asia. They only spread to the Mediterranean basin until there was enough agricultural development and human settlement to support them, and didn't appear into the more primitive areas of Europe until much later like 1000 BC. So their spread is only slightly faster than that of rats. Of course, there are wild mice who live in the Asian plains (and bush rats in Africa and other odd places), but then again these aren't filling any of the role associated with Rat - they're not invading people's homes and granaries to eat their food and spread disease. They are just small little animals without Aesopian personalities or mythic roles.<br /> <br /> So thematically, I'm still comfortable. By the time people invest &quot;Rat&quot; with these kind of mythic qualities, it is quite late in history - way after the Impergium and War of Rage. Paleolithic hunters aren't worried about some rare steppe rat eating their food surplus or spreading disease. People who live in civilized settlements in Mesopotamia and the Indus Valley are worried once the black rats and house mice invade their granaries and garbage. And the areas where they are prominent are lacking wolves, but abound with other animals of the Changing Breeds.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/544797-heretical-thoughts-on-rat?p=544940#post544940\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And it isn't like most Fianna ever ran across a griffin, unicorn, pegasus, or chimera running around in the wild... but those are all Tribal Totems.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm quite comfortable accepting that Garou meeting fantasy creatures like griffins, pegasus, and such at any time in their history precisely because they are made up and live only in the mythic past. With real animals, I still thinking the first meeting with their spirits have to be in areas where the spirit's children actually live. But even if they did not, so what? That Fianna might not have run into Red Talons until late (or don't) doesn't impact this topic. It might impact other topics for discussion, but I'm not interested in those (at least at this time).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/544797-heretical-thoughts-on-rat?p=544940#post544940\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1) Why are some of the Fera heavily associated with a small number of species while others are associated with vastly large taxonomical groups? Even without being scientific about it, it is remarkably odd that some are incredibly strict (Nuwisha are coyotes only) and others cover immensely diverse groups (Rokea are any sharks).<br /> <br /> 2) How exactly did the mundane animal associate Totems get where they are. How did Rat get to the top of its brood so fast compared to Mouse? How did Stag beat out Elk, or any other deer? What happened to put Owl in over Scarab?</div> </div> </div> </div>I think these are good topics, especially to those of us who do think heavily about the backstory and believe there is some common &quot;Garou Mythology&quot; that comprises their religion. I have no problems with myths being inconsistent because they are in real life, and I do believe that &quot;dream logic&quot; rather than scientific fact determines spirits, who their children are, and what other spirits make up their brood. How do myths treat them? Myths don't treat all canines alike. Wolves are distinguished from Coyotes in legend because coyotes do act differently even though biologically they are close enough to breed together. (I always thought Silent Striders are really Were-Jackals (especially those with high Pure Breed) who fell into the Garou Nation for whatever reason. Nuwisha didn't become part of the Pure Ones because Coyotes said fuck that, I'm doing my own thing). Sharks on the other hand all fit into the same generally mythic role because people observe them mainly when they attack humans.<br /> <br /> As for the second question, I assume the in-setting answer is that those spirits who were most successful in getting Garou and others to give them chiminage gradually became more powerful than others who did not and slowly assumed dominance over them. Garou saw Owl as being able to give them more benefits than some spirit like Scarab. So Owl became dominant. Rat probably dominated Mouse because as the larger, stronger rodent, it could boss it around especially after Bonegnawers and Ratkin made pacts with it, but didn't with mice).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-08T19:12:27-07:00","id":546344,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/544797-heretical-thoughts-on-rat?p=545909#post545909\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Seems a bit of a cop out though; especially since the cWOD posits that these creatures did walk the physical world in some fashion. This would presumably correspond to the areas with the strongest cultural myths regarding them. If the Black Furies can have Pegasus, because as some point flying horses actually did exist in the north east Mediterranean areas, even if they never fly around where the Fianna might have seen them, the idea that a Fianna never saw a true rat in ancient times seems pretty irrelevant.</div> </div> </div> </div>I actually agree with this. During the time when there was no Gauntlet and the Realm &amp; Penumbra are basically one, yes, there were real Griffins and Pegasi and Unicorns running about. For whatever reason, they did not stick around once the Gauntlet came about. Why did some animals remain in the Realm and others did not? Ultimately unimportant, but they did. Might be fun to talk about, but at least for this thread, unimportant.<br /> <br /> However, I don't see the relevance of it to this discussion. The reason I brought up that Fianna and Get of Fenris never saw rats is because it means Bonegnawers didn't live in Europe back then. They only existed in India, and only slowly spread to Europe - mostly following the migration of rats. Now is it possible that Bonegnawers preceded the emigration of rats? That they arrived in Europe thousands of years earlier than Rat? While they could have, I don't see why they would? They are cutting themselves off from their Totem if their children are nowhere around them. It is much safer for them to expand geographically only after Rat's children establish themselves.<br /> <br /> \u200bThere are a lot of details we just handwave in the setting. However, tribal rites and gifts do have material correspondences and such. And if you are someplace where your Totem's children don't exist, it may be very hard to contact him or get favors. You may say then what happens to the aforementioned tribes with fantasy totems who aren't around anymore, but precisely that they are now only spiritual beings, their spiritual correspondences are more important.\u200bThere are a lot of details we just handwave in the setting. However, tribal rites and gifts do have material correspondences and such. And if you are someplace where your Totem's children don't exist, it may be very hard to contact him or get favors. You may say then what happens to the aforementioned tribes with fantasy totems who aren't around anymore, but precisely that they are now only spiritual beings, their spiritual correspondences are more important.<br /> <br /> If we are looking for mythic themes, it doesn't make sense from a storytelling perspective to introduce an element that means nothing at that point in time. If you ran a historical chronicle in Paleothic Europe, are you really gone to have a Bonegnawer character when every other character in the game should be thinking, &quot;what the fuck is a rat?&quot; Or does it make more sense for Bonegnawers to show up after rats arrive in Europe? It's the same reason the Glass Walkers will given different Tribal gifts in the Dark Ages - to make them more appropriate for the setting","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-08T19:17:52-07:00","id":546348,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/544797-heretical-thoughts-on-rat?p=544856#post544856\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">RE: your fourth point, the Ratkin were not originally of the Wyld. They started &quot;falling&quot; to it after the War of Rage sent them underground. They aren't completely gone, but they do have Freak Aspects that are getting really close.</div> </div> </div> </div>If things like disease and famine were associated with the Wyld, then I'd have no problem with that. However, I associate those things with the Wyrm, not the Wyld.<br /> <br /> Besides, if we eliminate the original association of Ratkin with the Wyld my main point still holds - Ratkin are falling to the Wyrm right now because Rat the Totem is falling. I think that's a great story hook lots of people can use in their games. Recognizing their is a problem, figuring it out, convincing others you are right, and trying to save Rat - or any variation of that - could be a great chronicle for some folks, or even a nice evil easter egg for some GM using this in the background in a chronicle focused on something else.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-08T21:13:41-07:00","id":546445,"title":"","content":"Even before the Shattering, when spirit and flesh existed in the same places, they weren't the same thing. That's why so many of the mythical creatures are nowhere to be found by human accounting - they were so much more spirit than flesh, if they were flesh at all, that they fell on the other side of the curtain.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/544797-heretical-thoughts-on-rat?p=546348#post546348\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> If things like disease and famine were associated with the Wyld, then I'd have no problem with that. However, I associate those things with the Wyrm, not the Wyld.<br /> <br /> Besides, if we eliminate the original association of Ratkin with the Wyld my main point still holds - Ratkin are falling to the Wyrm right now because Rat the Totem is falling. I think that's a great story hook lots of people can use in their games. Recognizing their is a problem, figuring it out, convincing others you are right, and trying to save Rat - or any variation of that - could be a great chronicle for some folks, or even a nice evil easter egg for some GM using this in the background in a chronicle focused on something else.</div> </div> </div> </div>Except that the disease that gives the Ratkin their spiritual nature is Gaian, and the intention of Gaia for them to use disease as a weapon is Gaian, and disease itself is Gaian, and can be of the Wyld. The Book of the Wyld even went so far as to list Outbreak as one of its inspirations, and Possessed lists many infectious powers as being available for use by gorgons. Accordingly, the assumption that disease is necessarily a thing of the Wyrm must be rejected.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-09T00:11:59-07:00","id":546612,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>idpersona</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/544797-heretical-thoughts-on-rat?p=546207#post546207\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm almost positive that the Ratkin can already breed with other rodents. I remember something being said about maybe a squirrel Ratkin in the breed book, but without the book on hand I don't have a reference page. I can try and look it up later when I get home.<br /> Plus the Ratkin aren't just limited to the brown rat, it just seems to be their favored breeding stock in the modern times, probably because of it's ability to thrive everywhere. It's likely that Ratkin bred with various rats (and possibly other rodents) and when the brown rat invaded and took over, they switched over to breeding with them almost exclusively. The section on rural Ratkin specifically addressed swamp rats and desert rats and mountain rats. It is possible that in more recent times (meant as all recorded history) that Ratkin have been most exclusively rats and during pre-history were more varied types of rodents.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Now I want to play a capabarra Kin","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-09T10:02:22-07:00","id":547221,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://youtu.be/vkmf3Hbnh_4?t=40s\" target=\"_blank\">Crinos form.</a>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-10T04:08:33-07:00","id":548236,"title":"","content":"Hm. I'm sure I saw an article online somewhere saying there was a species of rat in europe some 10-15kya. Bugger. Welp, if the article was wrong, or I imagined it, or whatever, this could be something that sets the WoD apart from the real world since presuming there's no changes made to Shattered Dreams there were definitely ratkin around at the dawn of agriculture to piss off Garou.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.dropbox.com/s/ofii86boiyk38ng/DtDRandomForms.zip\" target=\"_blank\">Open the gates of Hell: Random Demon Generator.</a><br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1X6qQzoAPrSRNzl3cQcMWasoPv3eK56sIlAOTeay61No/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Gather an army of Rage! Random Garou Generator.</a><br /> Shapeshifting Historian<br /> Writer - Werewolf: the Apocalypse, Werewolf: the Forsaken, Exalted 3e, TC: \u00c6on","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/167-nikink","name":"nikink","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"360"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-10T08:16:40-07:00","id":548615,"title":"","content":"Overall I like a lot of the ideas the OP has and will think about incorporating them into my Apocalypse game.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nikink</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/544797-heretical-thoughts-on-rat?p=548236#post548236\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hm. I'm sure I saw an article online somewhere saying there was a species of rat in europe some 10-15kya. Bugger. Welp, if the article was wrong, or I imagined it, or whatever, this could be something that sets the WoD apart from the real world since presuming there's no changes made to Shattered Dreams there were definitely ratkin around at the dawn of agriculture to piss off Garou.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ok, the forum ate my long post so here is the short version:<br /> 1. DW Yalden in History of British Mammals (1999) states that black rats were in Britain by the Roman period with the earliest finds in London dating to the 3rd century CE.<br /> 2. Kim Aaris-S\u00f8rensen in Fossils and Strata, Diversity and Dynamics of the Mammalian Fauna in Denmark (2009) states that the earliest finds of black rats in Denmark date to the Medieval period (p.16) but that they should be expected to be seen during the Roman period as well. Brown rats are found at even later dates, but appear in the record in Schleswig-Holstein around 800 CE.<br /> 3. McCormick, in Rats, Communications, and Plague: Toward an Ecological History, Journal of Interdisciplinary History (2003) states that mt-DNA testing of black rat remains shows that they followed the spread of Rome across Europe (p.1)<br /> 4. Kovacs, in Dispersal History of an Invasive Rodent in Hungary, Acta Zoologica Academiae Scientiarum Hungaricae 58 (4), pp. 379\u2013394, 2012, states that the black rat followed the Romans through Europe and spread beyond the <i>limes</i>, with the earliest dates of black rat remains in Slovenia from 1100-800 BCE. He also cites other authors that discuss this spread: (Ervynck 2002, Audoin-Rouzeau &amp; Vigne 1994, Armitage et al. 1984, Armitage 1994).<br /> <br /> So that's a few things I found trying to keep to scholarly sources.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Lead Writer for the Savage Age of Werewolf: the Apocalypse: <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/428750/Ghost-Lineages-The-Advanced-Players-Guide-to-the-Savage-Age\" target=\"_blank\">Ghost Lineages</a>, <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/312700/The-Rise-of-the-Garou-The-Savage-Age-Volume-Two\" target=\"_blank\">Rise of the Garou</a>, <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/331302/Tools-of-Extinction-The-Savage-Age-Volume-Three\" target=\"_blank\">Tools of Extinction</a>, <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/352717/Tribebook-Wlewa\" target=\"_blank\">Tribebook: Wlewa</a>.<br /> <br /> Want RPGs in libraries? Check out my book, <a href=\"http://www.abc-clio.com/ABC-CLIOCorporate/product.aspx?pc=A3951P\" target=\"_blank\">Dragons in the Stacks</a>. Now with <a href=\"http://www.ennie-awards.com/blog/2015-ennie-award-nominees-and-spotlight-winners/\" target=\"_blank\">awards</a>!<br /> \u200b","edited":{"userid":1813,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1813-grimjaws","user":"grimjaws","date":". 06-10-2015, 11:23 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1813-grimjaws","name":"grimjaws","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1813_4.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"134"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-10T10:11:49-07:00","id":548922,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/544797-heretical-thoughts-on-rat?p=546062#post546062\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Alright, but I don't think that Rokea should also be whales just because some ancient people didn't meaningfully distinguish between them and every other large sea creature.</div> </div> </div> </div>Neither do I. Nor did I say anything that supports that slippery slope of silliness.<br /> <br /> What I'm saying though, is that the Bone Gnawers existing since pre-history works, because the cultural (and animistic) concept that is embodied in &quot;Rat&quot; covers things that aren't part of the <i>Rattus</i> genus.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Or we could cut the other way, see what's left standing, and build on that. I do like using fruits of research when they're available, and that was exactly the alteration made to the White Howlers in their tribebook, in stark contrast to 2e's REH Conan &quot;dwarfish brute&quot; Picts. </div> </div> </div>There seems to be a sizeable difference between, &quot;our knowledge of the ancient Picts has changed drastically, and we're making a White Howler book so lets update that,&quot; and, &quot;lets completely rewrite one of the major Tribes because the real world species most identifies with their Totem weren't as wide spread in ancient times.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/544797-heretical-thoughts-on-rat?p=546344#post546344\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm not unsympathetic to this, and I'm not trying to be scientific about it. I am trying to be consistent though. Rat to me are rats - regardless of whether they are black rats, brown rats, polynesian rats or anything else. However, Rat isn't &quot;all rodents&quot; which includes squirrels, porcupines, beavers hamsters, and other things that people don't conflate with one another.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ok, but that doesn't address two things:<br /> <br /> 1) We're judging ancient people by modern names (Nobody 5000 years ago was calling anything we call a rat today a rat). This is my concern about being too scientific. By just going with the true rats, you're cutting out lots of things people didn't differentiate from rats for a very long time.<br /> <br /> 2) Consistency isn't really functional without a framework. Consistent to what exactly? Why is consistency to the original prehistory range of the true rats a matter of consistency to other aspects of the game?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I think conflating rats and mice, which is what you are really talking about, is closer to the mark as people do confuse them and group them together. So it's a good point that &quot;Rat&quot; can be both the Totem of rats and mice. I don't think that's ever been stated in canon though (has it?) It's also true some species of rat are found elsewhere - yet clearly southeast Asia is home to most species and those that aren't are typically found in Africa (again, an area where wolves are not common). </div> </div> </div>Well, not just mice. And Mouse is in The Gnawer Tribebook as part of Rat's brood.<br /> <br /> You're also being overly restrictive with &quot;south east Asia&quot; the true rat territory includes most of central Asia as well (which means some direct overlap with wolves there). It's the Middle East and Russia/Mongolia that the true rats didn't make it into until people helped them out.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Of course, there are wild mice who live in the Asian plains (and bush rats in Africa and other odd places), but then again these aren't filling any of the role associated with Rat - they're not invading people's homes and granaries to eat their food and spread disease. They are just small little animals without Aesopian personalities or mythic roles. </div> </div> </div>I'd say this actually matches up very well to the duality of Rat in the books. The motherly aspect is more in-line with mythology from pre-agriculture around these rodents, while the aggressive plague rat is more in line with the invasive species that came later. Treating Rat as just one half of the presented entity seems to be part of my issue with this.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And the areas where they are prominent are lacking wolves, but abound with other animals of the Changing Breeds. </div> </div> </div>Except the Stargazers, Children of Gaia, Silent Striders, and Silver Fangs?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But even if they did not, so what? That Fianna might not have run into Red Talons until late (or don't) doesn't impact this topic. </div> </div> </div>It's a point you raised in your first post about.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> (I always thought Silent Striders are really Were-Jackals (especially those with high Pure Breed) who fell into the Garou Nation for whatever reason. </div> </div> </div>DNA shows that the Egyptian Jackal should probably be reclassified as a wolf actually.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Sharks on the other hand all fit into the same generally mythic role because people observe them mainly when they attack humans. </div> </div> </div>See... that I don't buy. It's certainly not a matter of consistency at least. Shark mythology is not monolithic. While the Rokea splats do cover the most general myths about sharks, what Europeans thought about sharks, vs. Polynesians, vs. Inuit, are all pretty different.<br /> <br /> [quote]As for the second question, I assume the in-setting answer is that those spirits who were most successful in getting Garou and others to give them chiminage gradually became more powerful than others who did not and slowly assumed dominance over them. Garou saw Owl as being able to give them more benefits than some spirit like Scarab. So Owl became dominant. Rat probably dominated Mouse because as the larger, stronger rodent, it could boss it around especially after Bonegnawers and Ratkin made pacts with it, but didn't with mice).[/quote ]<br /> <br /> I'd largely assume the same. But it also means that a lot of your original thoughts on Rat as a Totem aren't as strong. If Rat's metaphysical weight in its home turf (bolstered by early human lack of differentiation of similar rodents) was strong enough, Garou banding together around these tough little survivors that are highly adaptable as a value to form the Gnawers end up with Rat well before true rats spread more, and then Rat gets stronger and pushes the spread of true rats; rather than the other way around.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Might be fun to talk about, but at least for this thread, unimportant. </div> </div> </div>Unless there was a mythic plague-rat style entity of a similar nature to those that make up the mythical Totems; but was subsumed into Rat as rat's physical children stuck around.<br /> <br /> It's a tangent, but it's also a line of thought that addresses some of the initial topics.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> However, I don't see the relevance of it to this discussion. The reason I brought up that Fianna and Get of Fenris never saw rats is because it means Bonegnawers didn't live in Europe back then. They only existed in India, and only slowly spread to Europe - mostly following the migration of rats. Now is it possible that Bonegnawers preceded the emigration of rats? That they arrived in Europe thousands of years earlier than Rat? While they could have, I don't see why they would? They are cutting themselves off from their Totem if their children are nowhere around them. It is much safer for them to expand geographically only after Rat's children establish themselves. </div> </div> </div>See that's the thing. If being away from the natural habitat of your Totem's associated animals is a problem... how do the mythic Totems work?<br /> <br /> Why should the Bonegnawers not spread? Because other small rodents are in a different area that in a few thousand years aren't going to be considered rats?<br /> <br /> And it isn't like you can't just Summon a spirit if you need one. It's not hard to get in touch with spirits even if you're someplace they normally aren't.<br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You may say then what happens to the aforementioned tribes with fantasy totems who aren't around anymore, but precisely that they are now only spiritual beings, their spiritual correspondences are more important. </div> </div> </div>When does this ever play out as a significant difference/issue?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If you ran a historical chronicle in Paleothic Europe, are you really gone to have a Bonegnawer character when every other character in the game should be thinking, &quot;what the fuck is a rat?&quot; Or does it make more sense for Bonegnawers to show up after rats arrive in Europe? It's the same reason the Glass Walkers will given different Tribal gifts in the Dark Ages - to make them more appropriate for the setting </div> </div> </div>If they're going, &quot;wtf is a rat,&quot; something is already wrong because that word didn't exist for any animals yet<br /> <br /> Frankly, WtA has far, far, bigger issues with its Paeleolithic history than Gnawers being around in Europe before true rats get there. That's as simple as, &quot;you've got small furry gnawing things here, right? That's our thing.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nikink</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/544797-heretical-thoughts-on-rat?p=548236#post548236\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hm. I'm sure I saw an article online somewhere saying there was a species of rat in europe some 10-15kya. Bugger. Welp, if the article was wrong, or I imagined it, or whatever, this could be something that sets the WoD apart from the real world since presuming there's no changes made to Shattered Dreams there were definitely ratkin around at the dawn of agriculture to piss off Garou.</div> </div> </div> </div>There weren't true rats back then. They found a true rat ancestor (and ancestor of lots of other things) or offshoot of the rodents that went extinct. But pretty much every fossil rodent that doesn't look like not-a-rat, gets called a rat.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-10T16:37:35-07:00","id":549507,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/544797-heretical-thoughts-on-rat?p=548922#post548922\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There weren't true rats back then. They found a true rat ancestor (and ancestor of lots of other things) or offshoot of the rodents that went extinct. But pretty much every fossil rodent that doesn't look like not-a-rat, gets called a rat.</div> </div> </div> </div>Works for me. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Still frustrating cuz I tried to stick to historical findings.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.dropbox.com/s/ofii86boiyk38ng/DtDRandomForms.zip\" target=\"_blank\">Open the gates of Hell: Random Demon Generator.</a><br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1X6qQzoAPrSRNzl3cQcMWasoPv3eK56sIlAOTeay61No/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Gather an army of Rage! Random Garou Generator.</a><br /> Shapeshifting Historian<br /> Writer - Werewolf: the Apocalypse, Werewolf: the Forsaken, Exalted 3e, TC: \u00c6on","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/167-nikink","name":"nikink","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"360"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-10T20:13:37-07:00","id":549628,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/544797-heretical-thoughts-on-rat?p=546445#post546445\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Except that the disease that gives the Ratkin their spiritual nature is Gaian, and the intention of Gaia for them to use disease as a weapon is Gaian, and disease itself is Gaian, and can be of the Wyld. The Book of the Wyld even went so far as to list Outbreak as one of its inspirations, and Possessed lists many infectious powers as being available for use by gorgons. Accordingly, the assumption that disease is necessarily a thing of the Wyrm must be rejected.</div> </div> </div> </div>This will have to be one of many areas where I depart from canon. While what appropriately fits into Wyld, Weaver, or Wyrm has always been vague and contradictory, I think including disease as part of the Wyld just makes the Triat incoherent. I think the logic you mentioned doesn't work. In myth, legend, and folklore, disease is clearly one of those things that is thematically more appropriate to the Wyrm than the Wyld.<br /> <br /> I have no problem with the Wyld being dangerous - the wilderness is. If someone builds a house in a flood plain and it's destroyed in a flood, then I have no problem with it being blamed on the Wyld. But at some point, natural disasters switch over from the routine dangers of the wilderness to rare, apocalyptic events and those are the Wyrm (even if the game would say it's force of the Wyld <i>corrupted</i> by the Wyrm - lots of examples of that). That's why myths state that volcano over there erupts because the monster Typhon is trapped in it, or that earthquakes happen because some imprisoned evil god is writhing against his chains.<br /> <br /> Disease is even an easier call. When the Black Death kills over a third of Europe - people aren't saying it's because of the Wyld. When smallpox and measles (among others) kills 90% of the population of the Americas after Columbus, it's not being blamed on the Wyld. The Pure Ones aren't calling the other Tribes &quot;Wyldcomers&quot;. These are horrific events and most appropriately should be attributed to the Wyrm. Arguments otherwise seem like sophistry.<br /> <br /> If you like Canon better, great for you. I know however, there are lots of people who think like I do. Whether I am in the majority or minority, I don't know.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-10T21:11:42-07:00","id":549739,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/544797-heretical-thoughts-on-rat?p=548922#post548922\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ok, but that doesn't address two things:<br /> <br /> 1) We're judging ancient people by modern names (Nobody 5000 years ago was calling anything we call a rat today a rat). This is my concern about being too scientific. By just going with the true rats, you're cutting out lots of things people didn't differentiate from rats for a very long time.<br /> <br /> 2) Consistency isn't really functional without a framework. Consistent to what exactly? Why is consistency to the original prehistory range of the true rats a matter of consistency to other aspects of the game?</div> </div> </div> </div>I acknowledged in the post that I have no problem with &quot;Rat&quot; being the Totem of both rats and mice. However, I don't think that actually changes anything and explained why.<br /> <br /> As for consistency, I'm looking for consistent with myth.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/544797-heretical-thoughts-on-rat?p=548922#post548922\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You're also being overly restrictive with &quot;south east Asia&quot; the true rat territory includes most of central Asia as well (which means some direct overlap with wolves there). It's the Middle East and Russia/Mongolia that the true rats didn't make it into until people helped them out.</div> </div> </div> </div>My understanding is that rats didn't reach Central Asia until much later and that as a result of slowly spreading first into India and then China. When I did my research on this, it seems scientists thought earlier that brown rats may have originated in Central Asia and not Southeast Asia, but now Southeast Asia is preferred.<br /> <br /> If I find proof that Central Asia was home to these rats in the proper historical era we place the War of Rage, then I'd revise my alternate backstory to reflect it. If you can point to resources, I'll certainly check them.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/544797-heretical-thoughts-on-rat?p=548922#post548922\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd say this actually matches up very well to the duality of Rat in the books. The motherly aspect is more in-line with mythology from pre-agriculture around these rodents, while the aggressive plague rat is more in line with the invasive species that came later. Treating Rat as just one half of the presented entity seems to be part of my issue with this.</div> </div> </div> </div>I have no problem with the duality which is why I am trying to distinguish between the early Ratkin - who I don't think could act like the plague rats of modern imagination - and the canon description of Ratkin now - who are heavily portrayed as that. I think that duality is best expressed by stating that the modern Ratkin are falling to the Wyrm and going away from what Gaia intended.<br /> <br /> So on this part, I don't think we're that apart from each other.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/544797-heretical-thoughts-on-rat?p=548922#post548922\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Except the Stargazers, Children of Gaia, Silent Striders, and Silver Fangs?</div> </div> </div> </div>Not if we are talking about Southeast Asia which we have to be if we are talking about the time when the War of Rage happened. Those tribes will be among the first to meet any children of Rat when they expand as rats migrate with humanity. See my original point as to when rats first reached Central Asia. I don't think it's as clear cut as you think it is that rats were there say 8000-5000 BC.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/544797-heretical-thoughts-on-rat?p=548922#post548922\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">DNA shows that the Egyptian Jackal should probably be reclassified as a wolf actually.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, although I don't think scientists have reclassified them yet. This is another area where the question of where you draw the line base on science and myth happens. Everyone will have different preferences. But my comments on the jackals are an aside, so I don't want to distract our conversation here.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/544797-heretical-thoughts-on-rat?p=548922#post548922\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd largely assume the same. But it also means that a lot of your original thoughts on Rat as a Totem aren't as strong. If Rat's metaphysical weight in its home turf (bolstered by early human lack of differentiation of similar rodents) was strong enough, Garou banding together around these tough little survivors that are highly adaptable as a value to form the Gnawers end up with Rat well before true rats spread more, and then Rat gets stronger and pushes the spread of true rats; rather than the other way around.</div> </div> </div> </div>I understand what you are saying, but I don't think that applies in this scenario. At best, it means Bonegnawers are in front of the rat migration by maybe several decades or a century. I don't see them outpacing rats by any truly long period of time. These are the Bonegnawers after all, the low man on the totem pole. The omega tribe. It's going to be a long slog to get past those Tribes.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/544797-heretical-thoughts-on-rat?p=548922#post548922\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">See that's the thing. If being away from the natural habitat of your Totem's associated animals is a problem... how do the mythic Totems work?</div> </div> </div> </div>I did discuss this in the post. I suggested that for Totems that only exist in the spirit world that spiritual correspondences are more important.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/544797-heretical-thoughts-on-rat?p=548922#post548922\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Why should the Bonegnawers not spread? Because other small rodents are in a different area that in a few thousand years aren't going to be considered rats?<br /> <br /> And it isn't like you can't just Summon a spirit if you need one. It's not hard to get in touch with spirits even if you're someplace they normally aren't.</div> </div> </div> </div>I do think it's an obstacle. Maybe not an insurmountable one, but one that would delay the Bonegnawers significantly. As I stated before, even if we expand Rats to include all such vermin (black rats and house mice as opposed to just black rats - and I'm find with that if that's what you mean by rodents and not including animals like squirrels which clearly were never considered rats/mice), we're not expanding things all that much, at least not enough to make major changes to the timeline.<br /> <br /> In game terms, you roll the dice and the rite works. In the actual context of the setting, the Garou are doing a lot of detailed work to make that rite work - getting your material and spiritual correspondences. In actual game play, that's usually hand waved and ignored. If you take the setting seriously though, there are lots of things that can cause a rite to fail or become very difficult. If you are thousands of miles away from the nearest &quot;rat&quot;, and you try to summon rat will it actually work? Especially if you lack other appropriate correspondences? At the very least, it becomes much more difficult. And those kind of difficulties - among others - I think will delay the spread of Bonegnawers.<br /> <br /> We may all tend to think of the Bonegnawers as a Tribe that is always everywhere, but that can't always have been true. Traditionally, the Tribes had their homeland areas and didn't spread from it unless their kinfolk traveled to other areas - often by conquest. That's why we don't see Fianna in India. Or why the Get of Fenris didn't arrive in the British Islaes until the Anglo-Saxon invasions. We know the Bonegnawers aren't conquerors. They have to spread slowly. And we know they couldn't have originated in areas where there are no &quot;rats&quot; since contact would have to be made before the spirit can be encountered and tribal pacts made.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/544797-heretical-thoughts-on-rat?p=548922#post548922\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If they're going, &quot;wtf is a rat,&quot; something is already wrong because that word didn't exist for any animals yet</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't understand this point at all. They're obviously not speaking English. Of course no one in Paleolithic Europe has a word for rat - there are no rats there. But people who do live in areas where rats exist do have a word for rat. So what happens if the latter person shows up in Europe and tries to talk about &quot;rats&quot; - however they describe it - the rest of the people won't understand.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/544797-heretical-thoughts-on-rat?p=548922#post548922\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Frankly, WtA has far, far, bigger issues with its Paeleolithic history than Gnawers being around in Europe before true rats get there. That's as simple as, &quot;you've got small furry gnawing things here, right? That's our thing.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, they could very well say that. It doesn't mean they worship Squirrel instead of Rat. A Swara of the Bastet could show up and tell Garou, &quot;you got four legged predators here killing prey, right? You call them wolves. We call them cheetahs. Same thing.&quot; But they're not the same thing. Nobody would think they are the same thing.<br /> <br /> We don't need to be using scientific definitions here. I'm fine with however people used them then as long as we keep things thematically consistent and appropriate. Rats and house mice were not in Europe doing that time even if very different rodents were. If we are talking about Rat as a Totem, it's very clear what that means for a Bonegnawer. It's not a wild animal on the plains being eaten by birds and other predators. It's their presence among the trash of humanity - showing how these small animals have left the wilderness where they are preyed upon, yet thrive in the midst of humanity - that gives the mythic theme and purpose of the tribe.<br /> <br /> In my mind at least, this pushes the origin of the Bonegnawers far later than many of the original tribes, and that has implication for tribal and Garou history.<br /> <br /> Since you have different assumptions, you come to different conclusions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-10T21:17:00-07:00","id":549742,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>grimjaws</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/544797-heretical-thoughts-on-rat?p=548615#post548615\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Overall I like a lot of the ideas the OP has and will think about incorporating them into my Apocalypse game.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm glad you're able to find something useful.<br /> <br /> I post a lot of departures of canon on this forum to share them with other players. I get all sorts of responses - good, bad, confusion. It's good when someone else can use them in some way in their own games.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-10T21:29:02-07:00","id":549754,"title":"","content":"Bonegnawers weren't a Tribe prior to the rise of permanent settlements. Those settlements tended to be agricultural. Agricultural settlements began clustering and storing food, which in turn attracted rodents and other pests to the easy food supply. When trade becomes common, rats can spread further faster than if left to their own devices, but Ratkin have great capacity for migration. So even if in the real world rats took ages to arrive in the mediterranean region, in WoD a determined Ratkin could get to Europe with a handful of kin animals long before then. Should the animal kin die out the human relatives can still keep the Breed alive for a while.<br /> <br /> If some sort of massive purge took place it's even possible that the Breed that was present was exterminated, or at least forced to retreat back to safer locations for many more years to come.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.dropbox.com/s/ofii86boiyk38ng/DtDRandomForms.zip\" target=\"_blank\">Open the gates of Hell: Random Demon Generator.</a><br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1X6qQzoAPrSRNzl3cQcMWasoPv3eK56sIlAOTeay61No/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Gather an army of Rage! Random Garou Generator.</a><br /> Shapeshifting Historian<br /> Writer - Werewolf: the Apocalypse, Werewolf: the Forsaken, Exalted 3e, TC: \u00c6on","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/167-nikink","name":"nikink","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"360"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-10T22:49:34-07:00","id":549796,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/544797-heretical-thoughts-on-rat?p=549739#post549739\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As for consistency, I'm looking for consistent with myth.</div> </div> </div> </div>Which myths though? <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> My understanding is that rats didn't reach Central Asia until much later and that as a result of slowly spreading first into India and then China. When I did my research on this, it seems scientists thought earlier that brown rats may have originated in Central Asia and not Southeast Asia, but now Southeast Asia is preferred.<br /> <br /> If I find proof that Central Asia was home to these rats in the proper historical era we place the War of Rage, then I'd revise my alternate backstory to reflect it. If you can point to resources, I'll certainly check them. </div> </div> </div>I don't have any good resources on how old they are, but the following are all members of the <i>Rattus</i> genus, and have natural habitats in Central Asia: Himalayan field rat, Sikkim rat, Tanezumi rat, Turkestan rat. <br /> <br /> So these are all true rats, and all exist in parts of central Asia, and would have shared territory with central Asian wolves (esp. before heavy human settlement).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I have no problem with the duality which is why I am trying to distinguish between the early Ratkin </div> </div> </div>I think you're not really applying it to the Gnawers though.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I understand what you are saying, but I don't think that applies in this scenario. At best, it means Bonegnawers are in front of the rat migration by maybe several decades or a century. I don't see them outpacing rats by any truly long period of time. These are the Bonegnawers after all, the low man on the totem pole. The omega tribe. It's going to be a long slog to get past those Tribes. </div> </div> </div>I don't see the impetus to stay close to rats physically. As well, the Gnawers with their lack of links to specific cultures have the easiest time spreading. They can go anywhere, and find the outcastes that the other Garou don't want and claim them. Just like the Silver Fangs, as the alpha Tribe, spread across Eurasia and claimed rights to the best kin with zero regard for the biological range of falcons (even if they have it a bit easier given many are migratory; though that gets into weird questions like why the Silver Fangs never bothered with the North American falcons).<br /> <br /> I also don't feel this is really well supported in the mythology of the game. If my pack can have Shark as a Totem in the middle of Montana... it seems like physical locations aren't really that important to the Totems unless it is part of their Ban. Conversely the games don't have the Tribes line up to the physical animals associated with their Totems. Griffin didn't drive the Red Talons across the Arctic/through the Umbra to the Pure Lands to help save the North American megafauna.<br /> <br /> Correspondences are important to all this, but don't seem nearly as tied to specific living creatures (both animals and humans) as you're painting.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I suggested that for Totems that only exist in the spirit world that spiritual correspondences are more important </div> </div> </div>But what is this based on? It seems to be a construction to plug a hole; not something part of any of the relevant mythology.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I do think it's an obstacle. Maybe not an insurmountable one, but one that would delay the Bonegnawers significantly. As I stated before, even if we expand Rats to include all such vermin (black rats and house mice as opposed to just black rats - and I'm find with that if that's what you mean by rodents and not including animals like squirrels which clearly were never considered rats/mice), we're not expanding things all that much, at least not enough to make major changes to the timeline. </div> </div> </div>What about voles? Are you going to look at a vole, in the eyes of an ancient human, and say, &quot;well that's clearly not a mouse/rat.&quot; Because if we add voles in with mice and rats... that's Eurasia very solidly covered.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If you take the setting seriously though, there are lots of things that can cause a rite to fail or become very difficult. If you are thousands of miles away from the nearest &quot;rat&quot;, and you try to summon rat will it actually work? Especially if you lack other appropriate correspondences? At the very least, it becomes much more difficult. And those kind of difficulties - among others - I think will delay the spread of Bonegnawers. </div> </div> </div>You're adding difficulties in that aren't in the text. While Summoning does allow for STs to adjust a lot; it isn't phrased on these considerations. And you're being extremely restrictive on your concept of how difficult correspondences are in all this. While for a modern Rat Gaffling, you're looking at spiritual ones like survival, sabotage, theft, and disruption of order, and material ones like food, and worn out/repurposed/handed-down items. These are things that go everywhere; and they don't really change much if you translate them into the past.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Traditionally, the Tribes had their homeland areas and didn't spread from it unless their kinfolk traveled to other areas - often by conquest. </div> </div> </div>The Silver Fangs, Gnawers, and eventually the Children of Gaia and Glasswalkers/[insert era appropriate name), are all stated as being bucking this tread far before the spread of humanity allowed for most werewolves to be anywhere.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> We know the Bonegnawers aren't conquerors. They have to spread slowly. </div> </div> </div>Why does &quot;not conquerors&quot; translate into &quot;spread slowly?&quot;<br /> <br /> Whether this came first, or second as a matter of necessity, the Gnawers are masters of getting into places and at least getting tolerated by the locals as much as the lowest of the low are. Their Gifts and Rites are full of ways for them to rapidly spread to new areas; and their Tribal culture makes that spread easy as they're not worried about having lineages of kin to bring with them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So what happens if the latter person shows up in Europe and tries to talk about &quot;rats&quot; - however they describe it - the rest of the people won't understand. </div> </div> </div>No. They'll just go, &quot;Oh, you mean a vole?&quot;<br /> <br /> That's part of my point about how wide spread rodents are. Even when you cut out easy to separate ones (beavers, porcupines, squirrels, etc.) you're still left with every continent having a rodent of a similar size/shape/niche to rats and mice. No paleolithic Europeans are going to be confused for long about this &quot;rat&quot; concept, because you can just find a vole or a lemming and say, &quot;this.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Yes, they could very well say that. It doesn't mean they worship Squirrel instead of Rat. A Swara of the Bastet could show up and tell Garou, &quot;you got four legged predators here killing prey, right? You call them wolves. We call them cheetahs. Same thing.&quot; But they're not the same thing. Nobody would think they are the same thing. </div> </div> </div>Can we not be so dismissively hyperbolic? I've posted lots of examples of rodents in this thread that make this issue moot.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It's their presence among the trash of humanity - showing how these small animals have left the wilderness where they are preyed upon, yet thrive in the midst of humanity - that gives the mythic theme and purpose of the tribe. </div> </div> </div>Now. I'd arguing that the ancient Gnawers were more focused on the adaptability, survival skills, and cleverness of rodents; coupled with most having strong social bonding. And that the human focus of the Tribe came about as an adaptation to a changing world.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In my mind at least, this pushes the origin of the Bonegnawers far later than many of the original tribes, and that has implication for tribal and Garou history. </div> </div> </div>None of the &quot;original Tribes&quot; actually work by this standard though. The defining cultural themes of the vast majority of them are as tied to the beginnings of human civilizations as the Gnawers. Go back 7000 years and the highly cultural Tribes have no real basis in anything. 7000 years ago the people in northern Europe didn't have a culture that would make any sense to have the Fianna and Get running around. 7000 years ago was ~2000 years before the beginning of the Egyptian cultural that inspired the Striders. If anything the Gnawers have a far easier time because they're not tied to a culture, but a societal role (the Fangs have the same advantage). Every human society that grew to a decent size in Paleolithic times developed inequality; thus haves (Fangs) and have nots (Gnawers). Pretty much everything that defines the rest of the Tribes doesn't go back all that far.<br /> <br /> Like I said before, the Gnawers in Europe is by far not the biggest hole here.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-10T23:44:14-07:00","id":549818,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/544797-heretical-thoughts-on-rat?p=549796#post549796\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think you're not really applying it to the Gnawers though.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is true, and it is because I think the Bonegnawers are more tightly bound to a specific kind of myth/theme as followers of the Totem, than the Ratkin who as actual rats can take on other aspects. Themes of rats outside as survivors in the midst of men who feast on the trash left by others are not as applicable to the Bonegnawers in my opinion who major theme is being the omega tribe. Ratkin can be more general rats/mice/voles - although it doesn't seem like the canonical depiction of them does that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/544797-heretical-thoughts-on-rat?p=549796#post549796\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But what is this based on? It seems to be a construction to plug a hole; not something part of any of the relevant mythology.</div> </div> </div> </div>I try to rationalize the setting so it makes sense to me. If you don't see the problems that I do or don't care about them, then we are just talking past each other.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/544797-heretical-thoughts-on-rat?p=549796#post549796\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What about voles? Are you going to look at a vole, in the eyes of an ancient human, and say, &quot;well that's clearly not a mouse/rat.&quot; Because if we add voles in with mice and rats... that's Eurasia very solidly covered.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is a good example and thanks for making it. It's much clearer to me now what you mean when you say rodents.<br /> <br /> My comment on this is to go back to the myth/theme of rat as it applies to the Bonegnawers. Do voles fill the same role as rats/mice in terms of their impacts on humans? If they do, I'll have to think about it in terms of the earlier back story. If not, then it's another thing I'll discard as irrelevant. I'll admit I'm ignorant here as to the impact. My initial instinct is that voles don't have that kind of impact - and certainly not to the extent that it'll push the creation of the Bonegnawers significant from say the time large settlements appeared in India to way back to the time of the War of Rage (I'm thinking this is several thousand years between the two, or at best that Bonegnawers appear as the &quot;war&quot; is winding down).<br /> <br /> But after more research if I think you're right, then I will adjust the backstory I use in my chronicles. But it will make it less awesome. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/544797-heretical-thoughts-on-rat?p=549796#post549796\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You're adding difficulties in that aren't in the text. While Summoning does allow for STs to adjust a lot; it isn't phrased on these considerations. And you're being extremely restrictive on your concept of how difficult correspondences are in all this.</div> </div> </div> </div>Some of this I know is flavor text that actual STs/players ignore in favor of playability. I'm more strict about whether it makes sense in backstory, and in actual play I handwave everything because people want to have fun.<br /> <br /> However, I do think many things contemporary Garou take for granted were much more difficulty in the earliest days because ideas/connections are not made and thus aren't as strong universally. At the same time, the Gauntlet is low or non-existent. Ask me a different day, and I'll probably give a different answer.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/544797-heretical-thoughts-on-rat?p=549796#post549796\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Why does &quot;not conquerors&quot; translate into &quot;spread slowly?&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Because otherwise how would they spread? Deer are found everywhere, but Fianna are fairly limited. Silver Fangs are more widespread because they are the Silver Fangs, the alpha wolves who have the prestige, accomplishments, and Pure Breed. They are able to muscle in other territories. The other tribes do not. Certain tribes based more on ideas than ethnic groups are more widespread, but they also lack the numbers in any one geography - I think they only slowly spread.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/544797-heretical-thoughts-on-rat?p=549796#post549796\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Now. I'd arguing that the ancient Gnawers were more focused on the adaptability, survival skills, and cleverness of rodents; coupled with most having strong social bonding. And that the human focus of the Tribe came about as an adaptation to a changing world.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'd argue these things don't come about until humanity sees &quot;rats&quot; thriving in their midst by living off their surplus grain and trash. I don't see it happening to random field mice or voles. We're looking at the iconic rats/mice we're familiar with - and which we know spread relatively late - bronze or early iron ages.<br /> <br /> I think this has been a good discussion, and we've both made our stance clear. But I don't think we're coming to a mutual agreement. Thanks.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-16T22:10:14-07:00","id":557900,"title":"","content":"Grffin &amp; Rat <a href=\"http://mysteriousuniverse.org/2014/09/extinct-giant-rat-returns-to-machu-picchu/\" target=\"_blank\">conspire together</a> against Man!!!!<br /> Silly, yet somewhat relevant. <img src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/forum/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/><br /> <br /> More seriously and related now... I do believe some tribes took more time than others to arrive at the form we currently know them. Tribes may have formed, spawned camps that evolve in tribes of their own, while the root ones disappeared and so on. Bone Gnawers, as their own name implies, derived from scavengers and the bottom-feeders of many groups so to speak - the poor, homeless, vagabonds, drifters, ascetic mendicants, sex peddlers and many other groups linked associated with the seedy underbelly of society (and the tribe to a greater or lesser degree in contemporary times), have existed for at least as long as urban settlements and possibly longer, harkening back to the agricultural ones in some cases, much before rats or mice came in contact with western society.<br /> <br /> While associated with the Rat at present, it doesn't mean the Totem was always associated with the tribe through its history - or if so, that Bone Gnawer-esque camps could not have existed in places outside of Asia for centuries or millenia. Stray dogs gnawing at human hunters' leftovers have existed for millenia in fact.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1147,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","user":"Baaldam","date":". 06-17-2015, 01:32 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","name":"Baaldam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1147_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-04T18:47:15-07:00","id":1447508,"title":"","content":"How could damaged Totems like Rat and Griffin be potentially healed anyway, we've seen that it can happen even to fully fallen totems with the Redeemed aspect of Bat, and what might Ratkin Following a wholly sane totem be like?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26780-crazyjabberwock","name":"Crazyjabberwock","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26780_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"50"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-06T16:17:40-07:00","id":1447736,"title":"","content":"Actually, this line of thinking got me pondering something similar, what if Ananasa is a Manchurian agent of the wyrm, She spent ages being in its captivity, and her commands seem for the most part counter productive to Gaia's needs, while she's sure these have a far reaching net gain, these are a delusion the wyrm could easily have planted inside her. Meaning the Ananasi are unwittingly keeping her in a state of sickness because they refuse to question her due to their cult like mentality. <br /> <br /> It popped into my mind when considering their two biggest scale actions, the Extermination of the Insect Breeds, noncombatants, surrenders and allies alike, and their wholesale Genocide of the Neanderthals, the latter especially has zero justification ever given, and the Ananasi likely don't need one, if Ananasa commands it, then that's good enough for them. But since their Patron is only subtly tainted with delusion rather then falling to the throws of madness like Rat and Bat or Hatred like Griffin, it gives more insidious twisting, especially in the moments where they do manage to accomplish something properly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26780-crazyjabberwock","name":"Crazyjabberwock","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26780_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"50"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-06T21:04:37-07:00","id":1447760,"title":"","content":"Arise, necro-thread, arise!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}